/** \mainpage ChimeraTK-ControlSystemAdapter-TangoAdapter

The ChimeraTK-ControlSystemAdapter-TangoAdapter implements the %ChimeraTK Control System Adapter for Tango

\section Integration Integrating your control system application into Tango

Prerequisites:
\li The application has been written with the ChimeraTK-ApplicationCore library
\li The application MUST have a ChimeraTK::ApplicationFactory instead of a plain static application instance

To integrate the application into Tango, it has to be linked against the ChimeraTK-ControlSystemAdapter-TangoAdapter
library. Simply use the `find_package()` Feature from CMake. The package provides imported targets, so all you have to
do is to use `target_link_library(${PROJECT_NAME} PRIVATE ChimeraTK::ChimeraTK-ControlSystemAdapter-TangoAdapter)`.

\subsection configuration Tango Device Server configuration

As is common with Tango device servers, it is possible to configure certain aspects of the server through properties

\subsubsection working_folder Tango Device Server working folder
To run the the same device server executable with different configurations, use the `WorkingFolder` property. It should
contain a path either relative to the location of the executable or a fully qualified path on the filesystem.

If no property is provided, the working folder will be the folder that contains the device server executable.

\subsubsection attribute_mapping ApplicationCore to Tango attribute mapping

By default, the Tango adapter will export all available variables in the variable household of the application
to Tango as attributes. ApplicationCore's hierarchical names are translated as follows:

The top level Application module is separated from the rest of the hierarchy with an underscore. The rest of the
hierarchy is appended translating "/" to ".". For example, `/ControlUnit/AverageCurrent/heatingCurrentAveraged`
becomes `ControlUnit_AverageCurrent.heatingCurrentAveraged`.

\subsubsection attribute_mapping_file Complex mapping of attributes through configuration
More complex mapping and filtering of process variables is supported as well through a configuration file.

The configuration file for the extended attribute mapping configuration is called `applicationName-AttributeMapper.xml`. `applicationName` must be replaced with the name of your application.

\include example-AttributeMapper.xml

In the example above we declare a `deviceServer` with two `deviceClasses` and three `deviceInstances`.

There can only be one `deviceServer` per configuration. The number of device classes and device instances, however,
is not limited.

The deviceClass has a mandatory `name` attribute and contains any number of `deviceInstances`. Variables inside the device instance can either be mass-imported by using the `import` tag, where the naming translation as described above is applied.

A device class can have an optional `title` and `description`. Those override the device properties usually provided by the C++ code. If not given, `title` defaults to the class name and `description` to "ChimeraTK-based DeviceServer".

It is also possible to map individual variables to attributes using the `<attribute>` tag. The `<attribute>` tag has a mandatory `source` attribute describing the path to the process variable. Using the optional `name` attribute, the attribute name can be specified.

One can also override the description and egu of the process variable using the `<description>` and `<egu>` child tags of `<attribute>`.

The optional `<eventing>` child tag enables Tango event generation for the attribute. Its content is one of `off` (the default), `dataReady` or `change`. `dataReady` emits a DATA_READY event and `change` a CHANGE event, both on every update of the attribute's process variable. The two kinds differ in their payload, not in when they fire: a CHANGE event carries the value and its quality, a DATA_READY event only the event counter. Eventing is only allowed for readable scalar and spectrum attributes. An unknown value and a write-only attribute are rejected when the mapper file is read. Image attributes are not implemented.

\subsubsection command_mapping Tango commands

A device instance can declare Tango commands with the `<command>` tag. A command takes at most one input argument and
returns at most one value, both named by a process variable of the application.

The `<command>` tag has the optional attributes `name`, `trigger`, `inputArgument` and `returnValue`. A `trigger` names
a write-only `ChimeraTK::Void` process variable; writing to it starts the command. An `inputArgument` names a writeable
process variable, whose value is written to the application when the command is called, and a `returnValue` names a
readable process variable, whose value is returned to the caller. A command without a `trigger` treats its argument as
the trigger and needs an `inputArgument`. The command needs at least one of `trigger` and `inputArgument`. A command
which carries both a `trigger` and an `inputArgument` writes the argument first and the trigger afterwards.

If `name` is omitted, it is derived from the `trigger` source, or from the `inputArgument` source when there is no
trigger. The optional `inputDescription` and `outputDescription` child tags override the descriptions of the argument
and the return value process variables.

The input and return types follow from the value type and the data format of the named process variables. A scalar
`uint8_t` or `int8_t` process variable, an array of `int8_t` and an image cannot be a command type and are rejected when
the mapper file is read. A `ChimeraTK::Void` process variable may only be a `trigger`.

A call blocks until the application has answered: it waits for a new version of the return value process variable, or,
for a command without a return value, until the argument or trigger write has been seen. A process variable which a
command uses cannot also be mapped as an attribute.

By default, a write-only `ChimeraTK::Void` process variable which the mapper imports becomes an argument-less command
without a return value. The `autoMapCommandsFromVoid` attribute on `deviceServer`, `deviceClass` or `deviceInstance`
restores the previous behaviour of the import when it is `false`: such a process variable becomes a boolean attribute
again. An explicit `<attribute>` naming a write-only `ChimeraTK::Void` process variable is allowed whatever the option
is, so it keeps mapping the boolean attribute. A `<command>` of the same device instance which names the source first
rejects the `<attribute>`, and the other way round. The value set on the most specific level wins, and the default is
`true`.

\subsubsection device_state_and_status Setting the Tango device state and status

By default a device starts up in state ON and with the status "Application is running.". An application can take
control of both by naming a readable process variable in the optional `stateSource` and `statusSource` attributes of
the `deviceInstance` tag.

`stateSource` has to be an integral process variable, typically a `ChimeraTK::StatusAccessor`. Its value is mapped to
the Tango device state: 0 becomes ON, 1 becomes FAULT, 2 becomes OFF and 3 becomes ALARM. Any other value results in
state UNKNOWN and a warning is logged.

`statusSource` has to be a string process variable. Its value is used as the status of the device.

State and status are updated whenever the application publishes a new value. This includes the value which is already
available when the device is created, so a device starts up in the state and with the status published by the
application instead of the defaults above. A source which is not in the variable household of the application, or
which is not readable, makes the start-up fail.

 */
